ntity and  because of this i took the metaphor very  much to be a representation of  generational pain as burke says candyman  ain't a he he's the whole damn hive and  we meet several versions of the  character towards the end of the film it  turns out that cabrini green has been  the site of several racially motivated  crimes from white people against the  black community and this constant  feeling of anguish has caused a literal  tear in the fabric of the location so  that several vengeful spirits exist  there now again this is very much how i  took the film but the spirits of the  quote unquote candy men very much  represent generational trauma often  described by experts as being something  that manifests within communities we  witness how the idea of pain is passed  from generation to generation the elders  of the community all have their own  candyman stories which they then tell  the younger people about who then end up  seeing their own legends created before  their eyes which end up being inherited  by the future as well generational  trauma still has very little known about  it but research states that these  stories can often lead to anxieties  within a community that are further  fueled by their experiences there's a  reason that some people get nervous when  they see the police and according to the  studies even if they haven't had a bad  encounter with them before it can still  lead to a certain perception being  formed the website has carried out  several studies and it notes that the  condition isn't just exclusive to the  black community and it's also popped up  in the children of sexual assault  survivors and the grandchildren of those  that experience the holocaust i'm  veering off into a lot of outward  territory around the film but it does  explain why there's this idea of a  candyman that isn't just one person but  rather a collective throughout the movie  anthony becomes obsessed with this and  after he returns to cabrini green he's  stung by 